* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
* TTYGET()
*
* The terminal modes PTERM sets, as a field mark delimited array that TTYSET puts back.  Fields 1 to 5 are the manual's:
*
*     1  Is Ctrl-C treated as the break key?  (PTERM BREAK ON or OFF)            1 or 0
*     2  Is case inversion on?  (PTERM CASE INVERT or NOINVERT)                   1 or 0
*     3  The break character, as a number: 3 for Ctrl-C  (PTERM BREAK n)
*     4  The output newline sequence  (PTERM NEWLINE)                              the characters
*     5  The input return key  (PTERM RETURN)                                      its character code
*
* The manual says more fields may be added.  SD adds the other modes PTERM sets, so that a program that saves the settings and
* puts them back leaves nothing changed:
*
*     6  PTERM MARK ON or OFF        7  PTERM ERASE (0 as at the start, 1-255 a character, 256 none)
*     8  PTERM CTRL.FILTER ON or OFF 9  PTERM BINARY IN (Telnet)    10  PTERM BINARY OUT (Telnet)    11  PTERM TELNET
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function ttyget()
$catalog !ttyget

$include int$keys.h

   t = ''
   t<1> = (pterm(PT$BREAK, -1) # 0)
   t<2> = (pterm(PT$INVERT, -1) # 0)
   t<3> = pterm(PT$BRKCH, 0)
   t<4> = pterm(PT$ONEWLINE, '')
   s = pterm(PT$INEWLINE, '')
   t<5> = if s = '' then '' else seq(s)
   t<6> = (pterm(PT$MARK, -1) # 0)
   t<7> = pterm(PT$ERASE, -1)
   t<8> = (pterm(PT$CTRL.FILTER, -1) # 0)
   t<9> = (pterm(PT$BINARY.IN, -1) # 0)
   t<10> = (pterm(PT$BINARY.OUT, -1) # 0)
   t<11> = (pterm(PT$TELNET, -1) # 0)
   return t
end

* END-CODE
